      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm looking at this NDA for a new client and i'm stuck on the \"residuals\" part. can u explain what this specific bit means for me: \"Notwithstanding the foregoing, the Receiving Party shall be free to use for any purpose the residuals resulting from access to or use of the Confidential Information, provided that the Receiving Party shall not disclose the Confidential Information itself.\" does this mean i can still use the general coding patterns i learn while working with them in my other projects?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The residuals, in this context, refer to the intellectual property rights that arise from the Confidential Information. Think of it like a treasure trove of knowledge, where the Receiving Party gets to keep the valuable gems (coding patterns, trade secrets, etc.) but can't reveal the entire treasure chest (the Confidential Information itself). \n\nIn essence, the Receiving Party can use the residual knowledge they gained from the Confidential Information to create new projects, but they must ensure that they don't inadvertently disclose any of the original Confidential Information. This is similar to how a skilled craftsman might use the techniques they learned from a master craftsman to create their own unique pieces, but they must be mindful of not revealing the master's secrets.\n\nTo answer your question directly, yes, you can still use the general coding patterns and techniques you learned from the client in your other projects, but be cautious not to inadvertently disclose any of the Confidential Information. It's like trying to recreate a magical potion without knowing the exact recipe – you can get close, but you might end up with a different result altogether.",
